---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="About · Idimmachukwu Okoro"
	description="From Django developer to Staff SRE / Platform Engineer at Moniepoint — how I found reliability engineering, and what I do outside of work."
>
	<Header />
	<main>
		<h1>About</h1>

		<p>
			I didn't start out chasing nines. I began my career as a Django developer, building
			backend services and shipping features. My path into Site Reliability Engineering was
			born out of necessity: when our lone DevOps engineer moved on, I stepped in to keep the
			CI/CD pipelines flowing and the deployments moving.
		</p>

		<p>
			Somewhere in that stretch, my perspective shifted. I realized I was more fascinated by
			the mechanics of resilience, scale, and security than I was by the next product
			feature. I wanted to build the systems that allowed other developers to ship safely.
			That realization pulled me fully into platform engineering, and I never looked back.
			Today, my background in software engineering gives me a deep empathy for the developers
			I support: I know what it's like on the other side of the pipeline.
		</p>

		<p>
			A pattern you'll notice across my incident work: I rarely stop at the one report. If a
			single complaint reveals a bug, I treat that complaint as a signal to find everyone else
			quietly affected by the same root cause, not just the person who happened to notice
			first. I'd rather catch a problem before it surfaces widely than wait for the tenth
			ticket to make it obvious.
		</p>

		<p>
			When I log off, my brain stays busy. I play the piano, play chess, write songs, and
			dabble in animation. I'm also pursuing a Bachelor's in Business Administration
			(Entrepreneurship). Building reliable tech platforms and studying entrepreneurship
			scratch different itches, but both rely on managing risk and optimizing for the future.
		</p>

		<p>I share it all with my wife, Hannah Idimmachukwu.</p>
	</main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	main p {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		max-width: 60ch;
	}
</style>
